jwt_faqs=[
('What does a JWT formatter do?','A JWT formatter splits a compact token into its segments, decodes the Base64url-encoded header and payload as JSON, and makes claims easier to inspect. Formatting alone does not verify a signature.'),
('Does this JWT inspector validate the signature?','No. It does not check a signing key, cryptographic signature, issuer, audience, or your application’s authorization rules. A decoded token must not be treated as trusted.'),
('What are the three parts of a JWT?','A typical signed compact JWT has a protected header, a payload containing claims, and a signature segment, separated by periods.'),
('What do exp, iat and nbf mean?','exp is the expiration time, iat is the issued-at time, and nbf is the earliest time the token should be accepted. These NumericDate claims are commonly represented as seconds since the Unix epoch.'),
('Is my JWT uploaded?','The tool uses client-side JavaScript to parse the token and does not send it to a decoding API. Use only devices and pages you trust, and never expose production credentials in public logs or screenshots.'),
('Can it decode an encrypted JWE token?','No. This page handles three-part compact JWS-style tokens. Compact encrypted JWE commonly has five segments and requires a different decryption workflow and keys.')]
jwt_body=f'''
<section class="page-hero jwt"><div class="wrap split-hero"><div><div class="eyebrow light">Tool 001 / Token inspection</div><h1>See what the token says.<br><span style="color:var(--acid)">Not what it proves.</span></h1><p class="lead">A practical JWT formatter and decoder for reading a compact token’s header, payload, signature segment and common time claims—without confusing decoding with validation.</p><div class="page-meta"><span>Runs in your browser</span><span>No account</span><span>Decode ≠ verify</span></div></div><div class="hero-aside"><span class="aside-label">Anatomy / 3 segments</span><div class="code-large"><span style="color:#ffad94">header</span><span style="color:#747b73">.</span><span style="color:var(--acid)">payload</span><span style="color:#747b73">.</span><span style="color:var(--blue)">signature</span></div><p class="aside-small">A signed compact JWT is a series of encoded segments separated by periods. The first two are readable after Base64url decoding; the final segment is a cryptographic signature or MAC.</p><div class="code-band"><span>01 / HEADER</span><span>02 / PAYLOAD</span><span>03 / SIGNATURE</span></div></div></div></section>
<section class="tool-section"><div class="wrap tool-shell"><aside class="tool-explainer"><div class="eyebrow">JWT inspector / live tool</div><h2>Format the token.<br>Read every claim.</h2><p>Paste a compact, three-part JWT. The inspector decodes the header and payload, prints readable JSON, converts common time claims, and flags structural issues it can detect.</p><ul class="tool-list"><li>Accepts a raw token or a <code class="inline-code">Bearer</code> prefix.</li><li>Formats JSON and shows each encoded segment.</li><li>Interprets <code class="inline-code">exp</code>, <code class="inline-code">nbf</code> and <code class="inline-code">iat</code> timestamps.</li><li>Lets you copy or download the inspection result.</li><li>Never claims a signature is verified.</li></ul><div class="notice"><strong>Security boundary</strong>Decoding is not authentication. Treat every displayed claim as untrusted until your application validates the token with its configured algorithm, trusted key, issuer, audience and policy.</div><p><a href="/blog/jwt-token-parts/">Learn the three JWT parts ↗</a><br><a href="/blog/jwt-fields/">Read the claim reference ↗</a></p></aside><div class="tool-workbench"><div class="workbench-top"><h3>JWT inspection workbench</h3><span>LOCAL PARSING / NO API</span></div><div class="privacy-note"><span aria-hidden="true">▣</span><span><b>Processed in this browser.</b> The decoder script makes no request to a JWT service. Do not use a shared or compromised device for sensitive tokens.</span></div><div class="workbench-body"><label for="jwt-input" class="field-label"><span>Compact JWT</span><span class="field-hint">header.payload.signature</span></label><textarea id="jwt-input" class="code-input" spellcheck="false" autocapitalize="off" autocomplete="off" placeholder="Paste your three-part JWT here…" aria-describedby="jwt-status"></textarea><div class="field-actions"><button class="action-btn primary" type="button" id="jwt-decode">Decode &amp; format</button><button class="action-btn" type="button" id="jwt-example">Load safe example</button><button class="action-btn" type="button" id="jwt-clear">Clear</button></div><p id="jwt-status" class="tool-status" role="status" aria-live="polite">Ready. Paste a token or load the example to inspect its structure.</p><div id="jwt-results" class="tool-results" hidden><div class="results-top"><div><h4>Inspection output</h4><p id="jwt-token-summary" class="small muted" style="margin:5px 0 0"></p></div><div class="field-actions" style="margin:0"><button class="action-btn" type="button" data-jwt-copy="all" data-label="Copy all">Copy all</button><button class="action-btn" type="button" id="jwt-download">Download JSON</button></div></div><div class="result-tabs" role="tablist" aria-label="Filter JWT segments"><button class="result-tab" type="button" data-jwt-view="all" aria-selected="true">All</button><button class="result-tab" type="button" data-jwt-view="header" aria-selected="false">Header</button><button class="result-tab" type="button" data-jwt-view="payload" aria-selected="false">Payload</button><button class="result-tab" type="button" data-jwt-view="signature" aria-selected="false">Signature</button></div><div class="jwt-results-grid" style="margin-top:10px"><section class="result-panel" data-jwt-panel="header"><div class="result-panel-head"><strong>Header / JSON</strong><button type="button" data-jwt-copy="header" data-label="Copy">Copy</button></div><pre id="jwt-header-json">—</pre><div class="result-panel-head"><strong>Encoded header</strong><button type="button" data-copy-text="#jwt-header-b64">Copy</button></div><div class="segment-value" id="jwt-header-b64"></div></section><section class="result-panel" data-jwt-panel="payload"><div class="result-panel-head"><strong>Payload / JSON</strong><button type="button" data-jwt-copy="payload" data-label="Copy">Copy</button></div><pre id="jwt-payload-json">—</pre><div class="result-panel-head"><strong>Encoded payload</strong><button type="button" data-copy-text="#jwt-payload-b64">Copy</button></div><div class="segment-value" id="jwt-payload-b64"></div></section><section class="result-panel wide" data-jwt-panel="signature"><div class="result-panel-head"><strong>Signature segment</strong><button type="button" data-jwt-copy="signature" data-label="Copy">Copy</button></div><div id="jwt-signature" class="segment-value">—</div><div class="privacy-note" style="border-top:1px solid #dbe3ca;border-bottom:0"><span aria-hidden="true">!</span><span>This is the encoded signature segment, not a verification result. This tool does not have a trusted key or verification policy.</span></div></section></div><div id="jwt-warnings" hidden></div><h4 style="font-size:13px;margin:20px 0 10px">Claim-by-claim view</h4><div class="claim-wrap"><table class="claim-table"><thead><tr><th>Claim</th><th>Meaning</th><th>Value / interpreted date</th><th>Time note</th></tr></thead><tbody id="jwt-claims-body"></tbody></table></div></div></div></div></div></section>
<section class="content-section"><div class="wrap content-grid"><aside class="content-aside"><div class="eyebrow">How it works</div><h2>Read the data. Keep the security boundary.</h2><p>A decoded payload is simply data. Trust comes from validation performed by the consuming application.</p><a class="text-link" href="/blog/jwt-token-format/">Read JWT format guide <span>↗</span></a></aside><div class="content-main"><div class="answer-card"><strong>In one sentence</strong>A JWT formatter decodes the first two Base64url segments into JSON so you can inspect the header and claims; it does not establish that the token is authentic.</div><h2 id="jwt-parts">What are the three parts of a JWT?</h2><p>A typical signed compact JWT looks like <code class="inline-code">xxxxx.yyyyy.zzzzz</code>. Periods separate a protected header, a claims payload and a signature. The first two segments are Base64url-encoded JSON. The final segment carries a cryptographic value computed over the protected header and payload for the relevant signing algorithm.</p><div class="data-table-wrap"><table class="data-table"><thead><tr><th>Part</th><th>Usually contains</th><th>What the inspector can tell you</th></tr></thead><tbody><tr><td>Header</td><td>Algorithm identifier and token type; sometimes key ID.</td><td>Which header values are present, not whether they should be trusted.</td></tr><tr><td>Payload</td><td>Claims about a subject, issuer, audience or application-specific data.</td><td>Readable JSON and common timestamp interpretations.</td></tr><tr><td>Signature</td><td>MAC or digital signature bytes encoded for compact transport.</td><td>The existence and length of the encoded segment—not its validity.</td></tr></tbody></table></div><h2 id="decode-vs-verify">Decode vs. verify: why the difference matters</h2><p>Base64url encoding is not encryption. Anyone who obtains an ordinary signed JWS token can usually decode its header and payload. A signature is intended to let a verifier check integrity and authenticity, but a formatter that does not have the correct verification key and policy cannot do that check.</p><p>For production validation, use a maintained JWT library. Configure accepted algorithms in your application, verify the signature using a trusted key, and validate the expected issuer, audience, time claims and authorization rules. Never let the token's untrusted <code class="inline-code">alg</code> header decide the application's allowed verification algorithms.</p><h2 id="claims">What the time claims mean</h2><p><code class="inline-code">exp</code> is the expiry time, <code class="inline-code">nbf</code> is the not-before time, and <code class="inline-code">iat</code> is the issued-at time. These common JWT time values use NumericDate, typically seconds from 1 January 1970 UTC. Applications may apply a small clock-skew allowance, and the exact validation policy is application-specific.</p><h2 id="errors">Common decode errors</h2><ul><li><b>Wrong number of segments:</b> a standard compact JWS JWT has three parts. An encrypted compact JWE often has five.</li><li><b>Invalid Base64url:</b> check for missing characters, pasted quote marks inside the token, or characters altered during copying.</li><li><b>Invalid JSON:</b> a segment may decode to bytes but not to a JSON object. The token may be truncated, malformed or a different format.</li><li><b>Expired timestamp:</b> the local clock comparison is a clue, not proof of the application's full validation result.</li></ul><p>For field-by-field meanings, continue to the <a href="/blog/jwt-fields/">JWT claims reference</a>. If you want the anatomy and encoding explained first, read <a href="/blog/jwt-token-parts/">JWT token parts</a>.</p></div></div></section>
<section class="faq-wrap"><div class="wrap"><div><div class="eyebrow">FAQ / JWT</div><h2>Questions people ask about JWT formatters.</h2><p class="muted small">Clear answers for decoding, formatting and safe validation.</p></div><div class="faq-list">{''.join(f'<details><summary>{esc(q)}</summary><p>{esc(a)}</p></details>' for q,a in jwt_faqs)}</div></div></section>
<section class="section white tight"><div class="wrap"><div class="cta-panel"><div><div class="eyebrow">Next / Learn the fields</div><h2>Every claim has a job.</h2><p>Read what each registered claim means, which values are optional, and what a receiving service must decide for itself.</p></div><a class="btn btn-dark" href="/blog/jwt-fields/">Open the claims guide ↗</a></div></div></section>'''
jwt_schema={"@context":"https://schema.org","@type":"WebApplication","name":"JWT Inspector & Formatter","url":BASE+"/jwt-tools/","applicationCategory":"DeveloperApplication","operatingSystem":"Any modern browser","isAccessibleForFree":True,"browserRequirements":"JavaScript enabled","description":"Decode and format the header and payload of a compact JWT locally in the browser. Does not verify signatures."}
write('jwt-tools/index.html',page('JWT Formatter & Decoder — Inspect Header, Payload and Claims | DevArea Tools','Free browser-based JWT formatter and decoder. Inspect header, payload, signature segment, exp, nbf and iat claims, format the JSON, and understand the limits of decoding.','/jwt-tools/',jwt_body,active='/jwt-tools/',schemas=[org_schema(),jwt_schema,faq_schema(jwt_faqs),breadcrumb_schema([('Home','/'),('JWT inspector','/jwt-tools/')])],extra_scripts=['/assets/core.js','/assets/jwt.js']))

